Title: The Greatest Puzzle of Them All (Judge cover puzzle)
Date: 1924-11-15
Date-Note: printed; archive.org metadata says 1924-11-08
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1924-11-08_87/page/n0/mode/1up
Clue-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1924-11-08_87/page/n32/mode/1up
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1924-11-15_87/page/n34/mode/1up


#MOLAR#PASSE#
HAREM#M#SHOPS
ADAM#GAS#OBIT
RE#OER#TAR#CO
E#ENVELOPES#P
#LX#ATILT#ON#
TO#M#ALE#G#OH
A#CAT#L#MAN#I
NO#D#PIE#B#IT
#FA#PIETY#AS#
S#PLEASURES#A
PT#ANN#DSC#AB
ARAB#ODE#LACE
NAMES#O#TACIT
#POLES#FATED#


A1. Something she uses when eating bonbons. ~ MOLAR
A5. Out of date (fr). ~ PASSE
A10. A Turkish delight. ~ HAREM
A12. Why the dear things like Fifth avenue. ~ SHOPS
A14. The first man who couldn't say "no." ~ ADAM
A15. Something they step on. ~ GAS
A17. A press notice of yourself you'll never read. ~ OBIT
A18. A musical note. ~ RE
A19. Over. ~ OER
A21. The boy who has a girl in every port. ~ TAR
A23. If three's a crowd—what's two? (abr.) ~ CO
A24. Something the dears wear underneath. ~ ENVELOPES
A27. Three score (Roman). ~ LX
A28. How she likes to wear her hats these days. ~ ATILT
A29. Where she keeps her hat in church. ~ ON
A31. A preposition. ~ TO
A33. A drink—she prefers ice cream sodas. ~ ALE
A35. An exclamation—she uses this often. ~ OH
A37. What she calls any woman who disapproves. ~ CAT
A38. She takes one for better or worse. ~ MAN
A39. What she says—but seldom means. ~ NO
A41. A dessert. ~ PIE
A43. A pronoun—what she calls some one she doesn't like. ~ IT
A44. A note—musical. ~ FA
A46. Modern girls aren't so strong for this. ~ PIETY
A48. In like manner. ~ AS
A50. What she likes to go in for evenings. ~ PLEASURES
A54. What her boy friend carries on his hip (abr.). ~ PT
A56. A girl's name. ~ ANN
A57. What you'd deserve if you disagreed with her. ~ DSC
A58. The first two letters of the alphabet. ~ AB
A59. The sheik happened to be one of these. ~ ARAB
A61. Did you ever sit down and write her one? ~ ODE
A63. Something to do with dressing—the modern girl seems to have discarded. ~ LACE
A65. Something they change—bless 'em! ~ NAMES
A67. Consent implied—but not stated outright. ~ TACIT
A68. The inhabitants of a nation in Europe. ~ POLES
A69. Destined. ~ FATED

D1. Assured of success. ~ MADE
D2. An Anglo-Saxon money of account (better try the horizontals). ~ ORA
D3. This girl is not one. ~ LEMON
D4. Very early. ~ AM
D6. Similar to. ~ AS
D7. Where the good-looking lifeguard takes her. ~ SHORE
D8. What she does when she wants to get her way. ~ SOB
D9. Heroic narrative. ~ EPIC
D10. He has long ears (not a donkey). ~ HARE
D11. She has lost her job as chaperone. ~ MA
D13. What she says when you first try to kiss her. ~ STOP
D15. What they might have named her if she were German. ~ GRETA
D16. What she did to the poor boy's heart. ~ STOLE
D20. A character in "Uncle Tom's Cabin." ~ EVA
D22. She gets this way early. ~ APT
D24. A prefix. ~ EX
D25. Flowers of the valley. ~ LILLIES
D26. In like manner or degree. ~ SO
D27. Behold! ~ LO
D30. What she does not say at the altar. ~ NO
D31. If she doesn't freckle. ~ TAN
D32. How she gets when you are a few minutes late. ~ MAD
D34. What they do when they meet at the Sorority. ~ GAB
D36. What she tries to make. ~ HIT
D40. A proposition. ~ OF
D41. A musical instrument. ~ PIANO
D42. A study—musical. ~ ETUDE
D43. Third person singular present indicative of a verb (didn't you ever go to school?). ~ IS
D45. Associated Press (this will make you think!). ~ AP
D46. Where those who forget to pay their alimony go. ~ PEN
D47. What she hates to see come around (abr.). ~ YRS
D48. (If you found 48 Horizontal, this will be easy.) ~ AS
D49. To harness together. ~ SPAN
D51. To mark. ~ LABEL
D52. Striking (fr.). ~ ECLAT
D53. To aid. ~ ABET
D55. What she sets when she puckers her lips. ~ TRAP
D58. A hard test to stand. ~ ACID
D60. Love (Latin). ~ AMO
D62. Another way to spell dew (this is foolish). ~ DO
D64. They're never really safe till they're down and out. ~ ACE
D66. A direction (abr.). ~ SE
D67. She says this twice as you are leaving. ~ TA


Warnings:
Note: Judge's first cover crossword, printed over the cover art; clues in 'Key to Judge's Cover Puzzle' on page 32.

Printed with puzzle:
(See Page 31 for Key Chart)
THE GREATEST PUZZLE OF THEM ALL

